  "articleBody": "Framing a new wall can seem straightforward, but there are some common blunders that can lead to headaches down the line. Here are a few to watch out for: \n\n1.  **Skipping the layout:** Just jumping in without marking where everything goes? That's a recipe for disaster! Always mark your wall location and stud placements precisely. \n\n2.  **Cutting all studs the same length:** Your floor and ceiling might not be perfectly parallel. Measure the shortest floor-to-ceiling distance and cut all studs to that length to avoid bowing. \n\n3.  **Forgetting plate thickness:** When calculating stud length, remember to account for the top and bottom plates. It's a small detail that makes a big difference. \n\n4.  **Not checking for plumb:** It's tempting to just nail everything and move on, but constantly check with a level to ensure your wall is perfectly vertical before final fastening. \n\n5.  **Ignoring tilt-in clearance:** If you're assembling the wall on the floor and then tilting it up, make sure you have enough room for it to clear the ceiling. \n\n6.  **Underestimating load-bearing implications:** If you suspect the wall might be load-bearing, or if it's interacting with structural framing, it's not a DIY job. This is definitely a time to call in the pros, like HH Remodeling, to avoid serious structural issues and ensure safety.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
